[[:db/cas 17592186253459 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{{z}^{2}-z}=\\frac{1}{2z}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{{z}^{2}-z}=\\frac{1}{2z}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253462 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{b}-\\frac{7}{b(b-3)}=\\frac{1}{b-3}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{b}-\\frac{7}{b(b-3)}=\\frac{1}{b-3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253463 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}-r}-\\frac{1}{r-1}=\\frac{1}{3r}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}-r}-\\frac{1}{r-1}=\\frac{1}{3r}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253464 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2{h}^{2}+6h}=\\frac{1}{h+3}.$" "Solve the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2{h}^{2}+6h}=\\frac{1}{h+3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253494 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253501 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}+4$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” is true. The integral in statement “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” and “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” are true." "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}+4$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. The integral in statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” and “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186253504 :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” is true. The integral in statement “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements “${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” and “${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent” are true." "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is convergent” is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” is true. The integral in statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” and “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is divergent” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186253505 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253506 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253507 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253508 :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={x}^{8}?$" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={x}^{8}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253510 :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{y}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}={y}^{5}?$" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{y}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}={y}^{5}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253523 :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }=0$ is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$ is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$; ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ only." "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }=0\n$$ is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }.$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$ is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$; ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186253548 :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}?$" "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253551 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253551 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/11/11c74897fe914599a78c5997767bad83d13ddfb1e35ee66622d8880743688bad.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/11/11c74897fe914599a78c5997767bad83d13ddfb1e35ee66622d8880743688bad.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253570 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253575 :question/problem "$\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{4}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{4}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253576 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.$" "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta