{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{140}^{∘}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{70}^{∘}}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253636 :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}.$" "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253643 :question/problem "$8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle 8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{32}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right)=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186253648 :question/problem "Let \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} \\\\ 7y\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} \\\\ 7y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186253660 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256388 :question/problem "Simplify $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }.$" "Simplify $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256392 :question/problem "Express $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)$ as a single trigonometric ratio." "Express $$\n\\displaystyle 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [:db/cas 17592186256394 :question/problem "Express $\\frac{5}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡{38}^{∘}-\\frac{5}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡{38}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." "Express $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡{38}^{∘}-\\frac{5}{2}{sin}^{2}⁡{38}^{∘}\n$$ as a single trigonometric ratio."] [:db/cas 17592186256399 :question/problem "Find the exact value of ${cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right).$" "Find the exact value of $$\n\\displaystyle {cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right)-{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256406 :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$" "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256438 :question/problem "$\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256439 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =3-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot 270=24\\,300.\n$$" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =3-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot 270=24\\,300.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256440 :question/problem "$\\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256444 :question/problem "$\\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{tan}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256482 :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{t}-t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{6}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3/2}-3{t}^{2}=0 \\\\ {t}^{3/2}(4-3{t}^{1/2}) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{16}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{16}{9}\\,\\text{s}.$" "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{t}-t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{6}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}=0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3/2}-3{t}^{2}=0 \\\\ {t}^{3/2}(4-3{t}^{1/2}) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{16}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{16}{9}\\,\\text{s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256502 :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=\\frac{3}{4}$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186256505 :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=5$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,5].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(5)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(1)=2,$ and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=1$ to $t=5.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(5)=2+6=8.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=5$ is $x=8.$" "The position of the particle at $t=5$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,5].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(5)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(1)=2,$ and we can compute the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=1$ to $t=5.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(5)=2+6=8.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=5$ is $x=8.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256506 :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=1,$ and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=1+4=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=5.$" "The position of the particle at $t=7$ is equal to its position at $t=0$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [0,7].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(7)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(0)=1,$ and we can compute the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=0$ to $t=7.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{7}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(7)=1+4=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=7$ is $x=5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256507 :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=9$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,9].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(9)=x(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{9}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=5,$ and we can compute the integral ${\\int }_{2}^{9}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=9.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{9}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(9)=5+2=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=9$ is $x=7.$" "The position of the particle at $t=9$ is equal to its position at $t=2$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [2,9].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(9)=x(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{9}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nWe are told that $x(2)=5,$ and we can compute the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{9}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ by finding the signed area under the graph from $t=2$ to $t=9.$ Computing the signed area, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{9}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx(9)=5+2=7.\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=9$ is $x=7.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256626 :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+36{e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+36{e}^{6x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186256627 :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+400{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+400{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186256628 :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186256629 :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{3-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{3-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186256630 :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186256631 :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186256657 :question/problem "![](images/6c/6cabe5598368b4f26ccca515d4f4011100e0bc410b72d919db9681b3145814fb.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,{e}^{2}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" "![](images/6c/6cabe5598368b4f26ccca515d4f4011100e0bc410b72d919db9681b3145814fb.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,{e}^{2}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186256667 :question/problem "![](images/02/02582b9146af7bfa73ade5c4bd26ec7102650f324fede161f98482153730e868.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x={y}^{2}$ from $(1,1)$ to $(6,\\sqrt{6}),$ as shown above." "![](images/02/02582b9146af7bfa73ade5c4bd26ec7102650f324fede161f98482153730e868.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x={y}^{2}$ from $(1,1)$ to $(6,\\sqrt{6}),$ as shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186256674 :question/problem "![](images/46/46f2f23c1cc2cb2caacd3703665af673a52a7e876665c7ba9420ae72138e1068.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n${\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s,$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ from $(2,1)$ to $(1,2),$ as shown above." "![](images/46/46f2f23c1cc2cb2caacd3703665af673a52a7e876665c7ba9420ae72138e1068.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ from $(2,1)$ to $(1,2),$ as shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186256692 :question/worked-solution "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{6\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-6 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-6 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6)≈{80.538}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${80.538}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={80.538}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we subtract ${180}^{∘}$ from the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={80.538}^{∘}-{180}^{∘}≈-{99.462}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $80.538\\cdot (-99.462)≈-8\\,010.$" "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{6\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-6 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x-6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-6 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6)≈{80.538}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${80.538}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={80.538}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we subtract ${180}^{∘}$ from the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={80.538}^{∘}-{180}^{∘}≈-{99.462}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $80.538\\cdot (-99.462)≈-8\\,010.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256713 :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a46390ced6cc52c535f9f48e5e157cea83960caa339e98b23e3b64c93e0ea38.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." "![](images/1a/1a46390ced6cc52c535f9f48e5e157cea83960caa339e98b23e3b64c93e0ea38.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186256714 :question/problem "![](images/d8/d82f445e19af6e6d343bfab6c082108e945a8feb21caa9b60a2d93da24fc3f6a.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." "![](images/d8/d82f445e19af6e6d343bfab6c082108e945a8feb21caa9b60a2d93da24fc3f6a.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186256715 :question/problem "![](images/e4/e41579712730d26c66d9f46f8d978fd51c8efbd76394c094113454e24b3aaff7.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." "![](images/e4/e41579712730d26c66d9f46f8d978fd51c8efbd76394c094113454e24b3aaff7.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186256716 :question/problem "![](images/69/69072873cb1f613ee9c2fa4a4caf3dc3409026c2a8310847b1c3dd890cc816d8.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above." "![](images/69/69072873cb1f613ee9c2fa4a4caf3dc3409026c2a8310847b1c3dd890cc816d8.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186256721 :question/problem "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨{t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 2.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨{t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 2.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256721 :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-4$ is false, while statement ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4$ is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=8,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨16{u}^{4}-12{u}^{2},\\,2u⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$” is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx={y}^{4}-3{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4$ only." "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-4\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4\n$$ is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=8,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨16{u}^{4}-12{u}^{2},\\,2u⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$” is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx={y}^{4}-3{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186256722 :question/problem "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(u)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2\\pi .$ \n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by $$\n\\displaystyle {r}_{1}(u)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{2}\\right)⟩\n$$ for $0\\le u\\le 2\\pi .$ \n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256722 :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=2,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le \\pi .$” is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we note that both parameterizations traverse a half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ between $0$ and $\\pi .$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-1$ is false, while statement ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1$ is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1$ only." "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=2,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le \\pi .$” is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we note that both parameterizations traverse a half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ between $0$ and $\\pi .$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-1\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1\n$$ is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186256723 :question/problem "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 4.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3\n$$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 4.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256723 :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3$ is true, while statement ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-3$ is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=12,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨4u,\\,8{u}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$” is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3/2},\\,0\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3$ only." "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-3\n$$ is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=12,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨4u,\\,8{u}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$” is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3/2},\\,0\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186256725 :question/problem "![](images/39/39a7cb0b31cb5d906803063597ae968be8fba90f048a4d9745225de7c3f8d6db.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." "![](images/39/39a7cb0b31cb5d906803063597ae968be8fba90f048a4d9745225de7c3f8d6db.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186256730 :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7844777c0bb540ba337756f45c4d71a8a8e9f9c84372e01542892801efecce3.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." "![](images/d7/d7844777c0bb540ba337756f45c4d71a8a8e9f9c84372e01542892801efecce3.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186256731 :question/problem "![](images/68/68b3f78c29c9d7776efc2a78672ae53fe2e806d7ca6c3d663de8a16c03342132.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." "![](images/68/68b3f78c29c9d7776efc2a78672ae53fe2e806d7ca6c3d663de8a16c03342132.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186256732 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 1-(-4)\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 1-(-4)\\cdot (-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256733 :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf87c4707c0f4a8ab1b5157a13e8c7785da915833c5f9d896a2747af27f70e59.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." "![](images/bf/bf87c4707c0f4a8ab1b5157a13e8c7785da915833c5f9d896a2747af27f70e59.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [:db/cas 17592186256733 :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/ff/ff52b8e83a9cc9867b25ea3c24ed0f988a27dec73f9e0ab2dfda28ddabb3238a.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2\\pi +4\\sqrt{2}=2(\\pi +2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$" "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/ff/ff52b8e83a9cc9867b25ea3c24ed0f988a27dec73f9e0ab2dfda28ddabb3238a.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2\\pi +4\\sqrt{2}=2(\\pi +2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256734 :question/problem "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+1},\\,3t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+1},\\,2u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+1},\\,3t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+1},\\,2u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256734 :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-k$ is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{2k}{3}$ is false, while statement ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$ is true. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,0\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$ only." "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-k\n$$ is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{2k}{3}\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ is true. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,0\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186256735 :question/problem "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,2\\sqrt{t}⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 2 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨4u,\\,2\\sqrt{2u}⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,2\\sqrt{t}⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 2 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨4u,\\,2\\sqrt{2u}⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186256735 :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$ is true, while statement ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=2k$ is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2x},\\,0\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$ is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$; ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$ only." "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=2k\n$$ is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2x},\\,0\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186256754 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$S=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256759 :question/problem "Let $P={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}}$ be a partition of the interval $[4,11].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}.$" "Let $P={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}}$ be a partition of the interval $[4,11].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256759 :question/worked-solution "Note that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ is just the length of the interval $[4,11],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{0})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{x}_{3}}-\\cancel{{x}_{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}+{x}_{m} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =11-4 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\n$$ is just the length of the interval $[4,11],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{0})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{x}_{3}}-\\cancel{{x}_{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}+{x}_{m} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =11-4 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256760 :question/problem "Let $P={{z}_{0},{z}_{1},…,{z}_{q}}$ be a partition of the interval $[-7,-1].$ If $Δ{z}_{k}={z}_{k}-{z}_{k-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k}.$" "Let $P={{z}_{0},{z}_{1},…,{z}_{q}}$ be a partition of the interval $[-7,-1].$ If $Δ{z}_{k}={z}_{k}-{z}_{k-1},$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256760 :question/worked-solution "Note that ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k}$ is just the length of the interval $[-7,-1],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k} & =({z}_{1}-{z}_{0})+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})+({z}_{3}-{z}_{2})+⋯+({z}_{q}-{z}_{q-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{z}_{1}}-{z}_{0})+(\\cancel{{z}_{2}}-\\cancel{{z}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{z}_{3}}-\\cancel{{z}_{2}})+⋯+({z}_{q}-\\cancel{{z}_{q-1}}) \\\\  & =-{z}_{0}+{z}_{q} \\\\  & ={z}_{q}-{z}_{0} \\\\  & =(-1)-(-7) \\\\  & =7-1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k}\n$$ is just the length of the interval $[-7,-1],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k} & =({z}_{1}-{z}_{0})+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})+({z}_{3}-{z}_{2})+⋯+({z}_{q}-{z}_{q-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{z}_{1}}-{z}_{0})+(\\cancel{{z}_{2}}-\\cancel{{z}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{z}_{3}}-\\cancel{{z}_{2}})+⋯+({z}_{q}-\\cancel{{z}_{q-1}}) \\\\  & =-{z}_{0}+{z}_{q} \\\\  & ={z}_{q}-{z}_{0} \\\\  & =(-1)-(-7) \\\\  & =7-1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256761 :question/problem "Let $P=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[2,10].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}-4Δ{x}_{i}.$" "Let $P=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[2,10].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}-4Δ{x}_{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256761 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the factor $(-4)$ outside the summation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}-4Δ{x}_{i}=-4{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ is just the length of the interval $[2,10],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{0})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{x}_{3}}-\\cancel{{x}_{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}+{x}_{m} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-4{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}=(-4)\\cdot 8=-32.\n$$" "First, we take the factor $(-4)$ outside the summation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}-4Δ{x}_{i}=-4{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\n$$ is just the length of the interval $[2,10],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{0})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{x}_{3}}-\\cancel{{x}_{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}+{x}_{m} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-4{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}=(-4)\\cdot 8=-32.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256762 :question/problem "Let $P=\\{{z}_{0},{z}_{1},…,{z}_{q}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[4,9].$ If $Δ{z}_{k}={z}_{k}-{z}_{k-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}7Δ{z}_{k}.$" "Let $P=\\{{z}_{0},{z}_{1},…,{z}_{q}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[4,9].$ If $Δ{z}_{k}={z}_{k}-{z}_{k-1},$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}7Δ{z}_{k}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256762 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the factor $7$ outside the summation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}7Δ{z}_{k}=7{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k}$ is just the length of the interval $[4,9],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k} & =({z}_{1}-{z}_{0})+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})+({z}_{3}-{z}_{2})+⋯+({z}_{q}-{z}_{q-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{z}_{1}}-{z}_{0})+(\\cancel{{z}_{2}}-\\cancel{{z}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{z}_{3}}-\\cancel{{z}_{2}})+⋯+({z}_{q}-\\cancel{{z}_{q-1}}) \\\\  & =-{z}_{0}+{z}_{q} \\\\  & ={z}_{q}-{z}_{0} \\\\  & =9-4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k}=7\\cdot 5=35.\n$$" "First, we take the factor $7$ outside the summation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}7Δ{z}_{k}=7{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k}\n$$ is just the length of the interval $[4,9],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k} & =({z}_{1}-{z}_{0})+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})+({z}_{3}-{z}_{2})+⋯+({z}_{q}-{z}_{q-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{z}_{1}}-{z}_{0})+(\\cancel{{z}_{2}}-\\cancel{{z}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{z}_{3}}-\\cancel{{z}_{2}})+⋯+({z}_{q}-\\cancel{{z}_{q-1}}) \\\\  & =-{z}_{0}+{z}_{q} \\\\  & ={z}_{q}-{z}_{0} \\\\  & =9-4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k}=7\\cdot 5=35.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256765 :question/problem "Let $P={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}}$ be a partition of the interval $[1,5].$ If $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.$" "Let $P={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}}$ be a partition of the interval $[1,5].$ If $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256766 :question/problem "Let $P={{z}_{0},{z}_{1},…,{z}_{q}}$ be a partition of the interval $[-2,2].$ If $Δ{z}_{k}={z}_{k}-{z}_{k-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})Δ{z}_{k}.$" "Let $P={{z}_{0},{z}_{1},…,{z}_{q}}$ be a partition of the interval $[-2,2].$ If $Δ{z}_{k}={z}_{k}-{z}_{k-1},$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})Δ{z}_{k}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256767 :question/problem "Let $P={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}}$ be a partition of the interval $[5,10].$ If $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.$" "Let $P={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}}$ be a partition of the interval $[5,10].$ If $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256768 :question/problem "Let $P=\\{{z}_{0},{z}_{1},…,{z}_{q}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[-2,4].$ If $Δ{z}_{k}={z}_{k}-{z}_{k-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}-2({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})Δ{z}_{k}.$" "Let $P=\\{{z}_{0},{z}_{1},…,{z}_{q}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[-2,4].$ If $Δ{z}_{k}={z}_{k}-{z}_{k-1},$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}-2({z}_{k}+{z}_{k-1})Δ{z}_{k}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256777 :question/problem "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}}$ is a partition of the interval $[0,3]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}}$ is a partition of the interval $[4,8]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}}$ is a partition of the interval $[0,3]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}}$ is a partition of the interval $[4,8]$\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},\n$$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256777 :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ and ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ are just the lengths of the intervals $[0,3]$ and $[4,8]$ respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =3-0=3, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =8-4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=3\\cdot 4=12.\n$$" "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$ are just the lengths of the intervals $[0,3]$ and $[4,8]$ respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =3-0=3, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =8-4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=3\\cdot 4=12.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256778 :question/problem "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}}$ is a partition of the interval $[-1,1]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}}$ is a partition of the interval $[-1,1]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}10({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}}$ is a partition of the interval $[-1,1]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}}$ is a partition of the interval $[-1,1]$\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}10({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},\n$$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256778 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple and product rules, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n10{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ is just the length of the interval $[-1,1].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}=1-(-1) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $j,$ we evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j} & =({y}_{1}+{y}_{0})Δ{y}_{1}+({y}_{2}+{y}_{1})Δ{y}_{2}+⋯+({y}_{n}+{x}_{n-1})Δ{y}_{n} \\\\  & =({y}_{1}\\,+\\,{y}_{0})({y}_{1}\\,-\\,{y}_{0})\\,+\\,({y}_{2}\\,+\\,{y}_{1})({y}_{2}\\,-\\,{y}_{1})\\,+\\,⋯\\,+\\,({y}_{n}\\,+\\,{y}_{n-1})({y}_{n}\\,-\\,{y}_{n-1}) \\\\  & =({y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2})+({y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{n-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{2}}-{y}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({y}_{n}^{2}-\\cancel{{y}_{n-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{y}_{0}^{2}+{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n10{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.=10\\cdot 2\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" "Using the constant multiple and product rules, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n10{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\n$$ is just the length of the interval $[-1,1].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}=1-(-1) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $j,$ we evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j} & =({y}_{1}+{y}_{0})Δ{y}_{1}+({y}_{2}+{y}_{1})Δ{y}_{2}+⋯+({y}_{n}+{x}_{n-1})Δ{y}_{n} \\\\  & =({y}_{1}\\,+\\,{y}_{0})({y}_{1}\\,-\\,{y}_{0})\\,+\\,({y}_{2}\\,+\\,{y}_{1})({y}_{2}\\,-\\,{y}_{1})\\,+\\,⋯\\,+\\,({y}_{n}\\,+\\,{y}_{n-1})({y}_{n}\\,-\\,{y}_{n-1}) \\\\  & =({y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2})+({y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{n-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{2}}-{y}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({y}_{n}^{2}-\\cancel{{y}_{n-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{y}_{0}^{2}+{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n10{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.=10\\cdot 2\\cdot 0=0.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256780 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}3y\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $49{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}3y\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the ellipse $49{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?"] [:db/cas 17592186256781 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $8{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2xy\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the ellipse $8{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?"] [:db/cas 17592186256782 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-8{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse ${x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}=8,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-8{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the ellipse ${x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}=8,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?"] [:db/cas 17592186259488 :question/problem "![](images/57/570b00c901daedddc9fe05855e2df5fb2c24158320544b955bda651210d9f803.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}16xy\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=9$  traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(-3,0).$" "![](images/57/570b00c901daedddc9fe05855e2df5