one point, then \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has \n the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*" "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$ intersect at one point, then \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has \n the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186262882 :question/problem "Find the value of $|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$" "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262887 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3,0,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=7-2t \\\\ y &=7 \\\\ z &=7t\\end{aligned}\\right..$" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(3,0,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=7-2t \\\\ y &=7 \\\\ z &=7t\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262888 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-5,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3+t \\\\ y &=t \\\\ z &=7-t\\end{aligned}\\right..$" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-5,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3+t \\\\ y &=t \\\\ z &=7-t\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262917 :question/problem "Given that\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ y & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(B)=12,$ find the value of $y.$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ y & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(B)=12,$ find the value of $y.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262918 :question/problem "Given that \n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & q & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand $det(C)=28,$ find the value of $q.$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & q & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand $det(C)=28,$ find the value of $q.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262951 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{4}[3f(t)-g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩$\nand \n${\\int }_{0}^{4}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4,\\,-1⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}[3f(t)-g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ \ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩\n$$\nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4,\\,-1⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262952 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}[f(t)-\\frac{3}{2}g(t)]\\text{d}t$ given that ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,6,\\,-1⟩$ and ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨1,\\,-2,\\,-6⟩.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}[f(t)-\\frac{3}{2}g(t)]\\text{d}t\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\,6,\\,-1⟩\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨1,\\,-2,\\,-6⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262957 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262968 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-3,\\,9,\\,6⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-3,\\,9,\\,6⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262969 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}4f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,\\frac{3}{2}⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}4f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,1,\\,\\frac{3}{2}⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262970 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{3}{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,4,\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{3}{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,4,\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262974 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262983 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265705 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{4}^{0}f(t)+⟨3,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-3,\\,6,\\,0⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{0}f(t)+⟨3,\\,1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-3,\\,6,\\,0⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265706 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{0}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨5,\\,1,\\,20⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{4}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨1,\\,1,\\,8⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨5,\\,1,\\,20⟩\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨1,\\,1,\\,8⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265707 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{3}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{5}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-4,\\,-12,\\,14⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨2,\\,8,\\,-8⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-4,\\,-12,\\,14⟩\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨2,\\,8,\\,-8⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265708 :question/problem "Given that \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4\\,i-2\\,j+k,$ \n$c=u\\,i-2\\,j-2\\,k,$\nand \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,$ what is the value of $u?$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4\\,i-2\\,j+k,\n$$ \n$c=u\\,i-2\\,j-2\\,k,$\nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,\n$$ what is the value of $u?$"] [:db/cas 17592186265708 :question/worked-solution "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(u\\,i-2\\,j-2\\,k)\\cdot (4\\,i-2\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =4u+4-2 \\\\  & =4u+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,$ we must have $4u+2=0,$ which means that $u=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" "The dot product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the dot-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(u\\,i-2\\,j-2\\,k)\\cdot (4\\,i-2\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =4u+4-2 \\\\  & =4u+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0,\n$$ we must have $4u+2=0,$ which means that $u=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186265709 :question/problem "Find ${\\int }_{1}^{4}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{1}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{2}\\,i-\\,j+e\\,k$ \nand \n$c={e}^{-1}\\,i+j+k.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}c\\cdot f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ \ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{2}\\,i-\\,j+e\\,k\n$$ \nand \n$c={e}^{-1}\\,i+j+k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186265710 :question/problem "Find ${\\int }_{0}^{3}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=i+j-3\\,k$ \nand \n$c=i-j+2\\,k.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ \ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=i+j-3\\,k\n$$ \nand \n$c=i-j+2\\,k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186265714 :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}}.$" "Determine $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265726 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265743 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-15=2\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 6+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 3 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,3=2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 3 \\\\ -9 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-9=1\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 6+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,10=1\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and $B$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $A$ and $3$ rows in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-15=2\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 6+1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 3 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,3=2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 3 \\\\ -9 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-9=1\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot 6+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,10=1\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle AB=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265744 :question/worked-solution "Here, $X$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix and $Y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $X$ and $3$ rows in $Y,$ so $X$ and $Y$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{X}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{Y}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={XY}_{1\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $XY,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}22 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,22=5\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}22 & 9\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,9=5\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$XY=\\begin{bmatrix}22 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Here, $X$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix and $Y$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $X$ and $3$ rows in $Y,$ so $X$ and $Y$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{X}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{Y}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}={XY}_{1\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $XY,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}22 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,22=5\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}22 & 9\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,9=5\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle XY=\\begin{bmatrix}22 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265745 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265746 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265747 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+xy)=3{x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+xy)=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3{x}^{2}+y,\\,x,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+y &=0 \\\\ x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the second equation we get $x=0.$ Substituting $x=0$ into first equation, we obtain $y=0.$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={0}^{3}+0(0)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+xy)=3{x}^{2}+y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+xy)=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3{x}^{2}+y,\\,x,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+y &=0 \\\\ x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the second equation we get $x=0.$ Substituting $x=0$ into first equation, we obtain $y=0.$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz={0}^{3}+0(0)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186265748 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=6x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨4,-2,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨4,-2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6cx &=4 \\\\ 2cy &=-2 \\\\ -c &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6(1)x=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(1)y=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}+(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3}).$" "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=6x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨4,-2,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨6x,\\,2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨4,-2,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6cx &=4 \\\\ 2cy &=-2 \\\\ -c &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n6(1)x=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n2(1)y=-2\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{2}+(-1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(\\frac{2}{3},-1,\\frac{7}{3}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186265759 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265760 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265774 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,3]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,3]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” only." "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,3]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,3]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186265775 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,4]\\times [-3,3]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “${∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,4]\\times [-3,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$ $={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,4]\\times [-3,3]$” only." "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,4]\\times [-3,3]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,4]\\times [-3,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,4]\\times [-3,3]$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186265782 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/e7/e79856c97ab7e4dd27bb1f476fd911c2e7d77aac6ab54fe50597b3aafbca720c.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.$. This function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ and $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ are equals $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.$; $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only." "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/e7/e79856c97ab7e4dd27bb1f476fd911c2e7d77aac6ab54fe50597b3aafbca720c.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$. This function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ are equals $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$; $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D\\cap R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186265783 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/89/8944afb608465e49c7ae253f8e2518aa3d43b1c6e121dd91178ca1a67116a508.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions $\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$ and $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$ equals $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$ only." "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/89/8944afb608465e49c7ae253f8e2518aa3d43b1c6e121dd91178ca1a67116a508.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}.\n$$ Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$ equals $3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-5{y}^{4}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186265784 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/bc/bc6d7409dee2b5935d03839d32fc3aad23f08f1dd2c65eae496df8f995960b23.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}\\right.$. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ and $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ are equals to $x+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.$; $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only." "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/bc/bc6d7409dee2b5935d03839d32fc3aad23f08f1dd2c65eae496df8f995960b23.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ are equals to $x+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$; $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186265785 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/41/414a5c8c14d70ff08d607c2a755f565c147b8b5e691a8d38a9e7db5697ba9e48.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.$. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ and $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.$; $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only." "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/41/414a5c8c14d70ff08d607c2a755f565c147b8b5e691a8d38a9e7db5697ba9e48.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{2}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(0,3)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\n- Functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ equal ${2}^{x+y}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$; $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186265786 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[1,e]\\times [0,3].$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\frac{{y}^{2}+1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ over the rectangle $R=[1,e]\\times [0,3].$"] [:db/cas 17592186265787 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ over the rectangle $R=[0,\\pi ]\\times [0,2].$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ over the rectangle $R=[0,\\pi ]\\times [0,2].$"] [:db/cas 17592186265792 :question/worked-solution "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $A$ and $1$ row in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication: \n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-3=-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-9=(-3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,3=(-3)\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Here, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in $A$ and $1$ row in $B,$ so $A$ and $B$ are conformable for multiplication: \n\n$$\n{A}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $AB,$ one element at a time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-3=-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,1=(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,-9=(-3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, &  & \\,3=(-3)\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle AB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265802 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186265807 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,-2<x<0,\\,0<y<2, \\\\ 2x,\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,-2<x<0,\\,0<y<2, \\\\ 2x,\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [:db/cas 17592186265808 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$, where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+1},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,-2<y<0, \\\\ 3y\\sqrt{x},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<4,\\,-2<y<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$" "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x+1},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,-2<y<0, \\\\ 3y\\sqrt{x},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<4,\\,-2<y<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [:db/cas 17592186265